00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:02.629 align:start position:0%
 
some say that the german general  election in september is the most  important election this year i'm in  front of the white house  to talk with jeffrey rathkey about the  us perspective  what america expects from the next  chancellor and what each party  stands for jeffrey reyesky is the  president of the american institute for  contemporary german studies at the johns  hopkins university  thank you so much for making time for  deutsche welle jeffrey  so some say these are the most important  elections this year do you agree  oh absolutely the the bundestag election  on september 26th  is the most important election happening  anywhere in the world this year  in my view because germany is at the  heart of europe  germany defines in many ways the  direction that europe takes  and it's a crucial part of the  transatlantic  bond so this is undoubtedly going to be  a major event on the political landscape  germany has a very different system than  the united states we have several  parties uh not only two as here maybe  you can talk a little bit  why that matters sure a parliamentary  system  in which you have many parties  represented  more parties now than than ever before  in german  in recent german history it means that  any government has to be  formed as a coalition nobody has an  absolute majority on  on his own and therefore that means  compromise  so it means that nobody gets exactly  what they want  and it means also that people in the  united states who are  thinking about the impact on the  german-american relationship  have to take into account this coalition  dynamic this compromise which is going  to  characterize german politics in the  future as it always has  what does that mean for reliability the  the tendency in germany to have a  negotiated coalition agreement it gives  a certain amount of predictability  and i would say the problem is in some  ways perhaps  in the opposite direction because  parties in germany  uh when they form a government they have  a written coalition agreement which is  often dozens  if not more pages long there is a desire  to try to anticipate everything  you can't anticipate everything and so  it's that  that can give a lack of flexibility um  to  future actions because things that are  not covered in the coalition agreement  then have to be hammered out  anew and there can be a reluctance to  deviate  from those agreements so i think it's  it's more a question of  um having enough flexibility to react  to the way the world is going to change  rather than predictability about what  your positions are today  and for the next couple of months as you  are an expert  on international affairs maybe we can  talk a little bit about the  expectations of the three biggest  parties i think that  the chancellor merkel having been in  power for 16 years  there is an expectation of a certain  continuity  and there you see from an american  perspective  that the chancellor has been on the one  hand  concerned about holding europe together  the european union  especially through a number of crises  now  germany as the largest and the richest  country in europe  has the delicate task of trying to  bring together the ambitions of those  who  want more european integration and they  want it faster  with those who are more skeptical and  and in that sense germany is a  compromise broker rather than a  at the vanguard of new innovations  instead it's sort of a moderating force  and i think that when you  look at europe armin lashith the  chancellor candidate from the cdu csu  has has said that he will think european  and he will act european that he has a  european instinct  and so i think that would be something  to look for  from a government that he might lead  how will it seek to strengthen the  european level of policy making  and what concessions will germany be  willing to offer its european partners  in order to achieve that greater degree  of cohesion  will that be on the financial or the  fiscal side will that be in foreign  policy  or will it be in other areas as for now  it looks as you just said very likely  that he might be  the successor of angela merkel talk a  little bit about his take on the  transatlantic relationship  if you talk about the transatlantic  relationship the cdu and csu  have always tried to characterize  themselves  as as real advocates staunch advocates  of a strong transatlantic bond and that  has  held regardless of the president in  power in the united states there have  been  major ups and downs in the relationship  but that uh  that real um adherence to the  transatlantic pillar  um as one of the two most important  aspects of german foreign policy  on the one hand the united states on the  other hand the european union  is is at the center of the cdu's view  of the world let's talk a little bit  about olaf schultz he's from the social  democrats they have a traditionally a  closer relationship to russia gerhard  schroeder one of the  chancellors from the social democrats  was kind of the  mastermind behind behind north stream  two  what what is your take on olaf schultz  when it comes to the  german-american relationship i think  olaf schultz if you listen to  the way he has portrayed his view  of of the world and of germany's place  in the world  and if you look at the way he has uh  talked about  the biden administration i think he has  sought to  uh align them quite closely to to tell  german voters in a way i know how to  work  with the u.s administration under  president joe biden  and and i think that encompasses  not only some of the traditional foreign  policy elements i think we'll come back  in a second  to russia and to north stream too but  it's also about  the global economy what kind of  regulations should there be in the  global economy olaf schultz  along with the united states government  have talked a lot about  the minimum tax on international  corporations and that has been achieved  an agreement in the g7 context also in  g20  now it's a question of turning that into  national obligations  but i think there you see an area where  the social democrats  have tried to present themselves in a  way as  transatlanticists for the global the  digitized global economy  now when it comes to russia i think  you're absolutely right people in the  united states who look at the russia  policies  of germany and europe one of the first  things that they focus on  is the north stream 2 pipeline project  and  and i think chancellor merkel of course  has defended that project  and she has seen through an agreement  with the united states  about how that might move forward but i  think people still see that as primarily  a social democratic party  initiative and and the priority and  there i think there's just a  major disagreement uh in both american  parties the democrats and the  republicans  um are quite critical of north stream  two and  uh and and that stance isn't going to  change  then there are the greens there were  times when people  were expecting hoping fearing that  analina berbock might become the next  chancellor  what i think you see from an american  perspective is how strongly  the values orientation comes through  in the greens foreign policy vision  you can see that when you look at  questions that relate to china  and especially things like forced labor  and the  the importance of human rights including  the implications of human rights policy  for germany's international economic  engagement there i think the greens are  they try to go much further than than  other parties in germany  uh have gone thus far if you look at  russia policy  i think you see also a not only a  criticism of russian foreign policy but  more fundamentally  a a deep  not only disappointment but a deep  concern  about human rights in russia about the  nature of vladimir putin's regime  and about the implications that has for  stability in russia  and russia's role in its immediate  neighborhood and more broadly  in europe and in the world so i think  there you see a  values-driven alignment that is very  similar  to the way joe biden talks about the  competition between democracies and  autocracies as  one of the defining elements of the  international situation today  do you have an idea whom the biden  administration  would kind of prefer  well i think they would be careful never  to express a preference um  because you know there's there's there's  nothing to be gained by  uh trying to weigh in in advance of an  election  it can only backfire um and be used  against you  i i think that what people in the biden  administration  recognize is that any german government  is going to be the result of compromise  and concessions among the parties so  that's the first thing  is don't read the party program of one  party and assume that that will  that will shape um the germany  of the future but i think you can also  look at uh  these positions and see how germany's  foreign policy might evolve  and there i think you can find something  you know something for everyone if you  on the one hand the continuity and the  transatlantic commitment in the cdu csu  approach on the other hand the the views  of the global economy  that uh the social democrats uh bring um  or  the the values-driven uh  imperative of the greens uh program  so i think people will be looking for  new leadership new uh personalities and  trying to forge constructive working  partnerships  with whoever emerges you know we also we  haven't talked about the  the liberals the the free democrats uh  there's also i think a significant  likelihood that they might be part  of a future german government and and  there i think you also see  um you know a you see a bit of the  values  basis in the fdp's election program  and and i think it's a reminder  that this may be the most complicated  german government  that we've seen in recent history i  think we have to  talk a little bit at the end of this  interview about angela merkel after 16  years in office that's a long period  she shaped the understanding of germany  uh for a whole generation  generation i think also in the united  states she's very well known  what is her legacy when it comes to the  transatlantic relationship  how long do we have  let's say two minutes okay two minutes  um  so i think that miracle's legacy is  uh you know it is on the one hand a  commitment to preserving  a working german-american partnership  and a transatlantic relationship  you see this in the way she took heat  at the start of her chancellorship for  finding ways to work closely with  president george w bush  um america's popularity uh in germany  was at a low point when she took office  in  2005 and so you know it's important to  remember  that she has stood for a strong  partnership with the united states  even when it was unpopular i think you  see that as well  during the presidency of donald trump  although in that case you see merkel  i think drawing distinctions to a  greater degree  between uh german policies german  disagreements  with the united states while at the same  time recognizing  the essential importance of that  relationship  and then i think you also have to look  at her  commitment to keeping the european union  functional and together on major issues  that is a is hugely important for the  united states as well  chaos in europe is not good for the  united states that does not benefit us  and having someone who is able to take  the long view  and to foster a strong european union  and and a strong european contribution  to transatlantic security in nato  is really crucially important jeffrey  thank you so much  that shall be continued after the  election and then we discuss the outcome  and the implication for the u.s german  relationship i look forward to it thanks  so much

